// Not Deno's config — deno.json holds the real compilerOptions and Deno never
// reads this file. It exists for esbuild, which Vite transforms TypeScript
// through.
//
// esbuild finds a tsconfig by walking UP from each source file and stopping at
// the first one it meets. With no tsconfig anywhere in the checkout that walk
// left the repo entirely and picked up whatever happened to sit above it — on
// one machine a Svelte config at ~/CODE/tsconfig.json, whose `extends` esbuild
// then could not resolve (no node_modules up there), so `deno task build` died
// with
//
//   [vite:esbuild] failed to resolve "extends":"@tsconfig/svelte/tsconfig.json"
//     in /Users/danieljohnson/CODE/tsconfig.json
//   file: <repo>/client.ts
//
// naming a file the repo does not contain. Whether the build worked at all
// depended on where the checkout happened to live, and the error pointed away
// from the checkout rather than into it. This file ends the walk at the repo.
//
// Deliberately minimal, and both fields are here to CHANGE NOTHING rather than
// to express a preference — the emitted bundle is byte for byte what it was
// when the walk found nothing. useDefineForClassFields is the one that has to
// be said out loud: esbuild derives it from `target`, and at esnext it flips to
// true, which stops class fields being lowered —
//
//   -  constructor() { super(...arguments); this.name = "InjectError"; }
//   +  name = "InjectError";
//
// across eleven server chunks. That is [[Define]] rather than [[Set]] semantics
// and it is a real behaviour change, so it is pinned to the false the build
// already had. Flipping it is a deliberate decision for another day, not a side
// effect of ending a directory walk.
// package.json beside this file is the same hazard in Node's half of the
// toolchain, and the reason it exists: Vite asks the nearest package.json
// whether the tree is ESM, and finding none in the checkout it read the
// parent's "type": "module" — then, once that neighbour was deleted, found
// nothing and defensively named every server chunk .mjs instead. Same build,
// same bytes, different filenames depending on a directory the repo does not
// own. Declaring it here settles that from inside. It carries no comment of
// its own because package.json must be strict JSON.
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "esnext",
    "useDefineForClassFields": false
  }
}
